    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Oral hyaluronic acid probably helps skin a little: a 2025 meta-analysis of 7 randomised trials found significant improvements in hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth.</strong> The trials were small and varied, and the best-known one used 120mg a day for 12 weeks in 40 adults.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What improved:</strong> hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth. Firmness, wrinkle volume and water loss through the skin did not change significantly in the pooled data.</li>
        <li><strong>The dose:</strong> 120mg a day was the dose in the 12-week trial most often cited. Collabloom contains 100mg, slightly below it.</li>
        <li><strong>How it works:</strong> gut bacteria break swallowed hyaluronic acid into small fragments; in mice only about 0.2% is absorbed, so any effect is likely signalling, not delivery.</li>
        <li><strong>Serum vs supplement:</strong> a serum hydrates the skin's surface while you wear it. A supplement aims at skin from within over weeks. They are not substitutes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

