      <h2 id="works">Does oral hyaluronic acid work?</h2>

      <p>
        Modestly, on the current evidence, and for some skin measures more than others. The best summary
        is a 2025 meta-analysis in the <em>Journal of Drugs in Dermatology</em> that pooled 7 randomised
        controlled trials of hyaluronic acid taken by mouth.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pooled together, the trials showed statistically significant improvements in skin hydration,
        elasticity and wrinkle depth compared with placebo. Firmness, wrinkle volume and transepidermal
        water loss did not improve significantly. The authors flagged the small total sample size and the
        differences between the trials, so this is a promising signal rather than settled evidence.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-stats">
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">7</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">Randomised trials of oral hyaluronic acid pooled in the 2025 meta-analysis</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=oral+hyaluronic+acid+supplement+skin+hydration+elasticity+wrinkle+meta-analysis+2025" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">J Drugs Dermatol, 2025</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">120mg</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">Daily dose in the 12-week trial of 40 adults aged 35 to 64</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=oral+hyaluronan+relieves+wrinkles+improves+dry+skin+12-week+double-blinded+placebo-controlled" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Nutrients, 2021</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">~0.2%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">Estimated bioavailability of swallowed hyaluronic acid in a mouse study</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=gut+microbiota+bioavailability+orally+administered+hyaluronic+acid+molecular+weight" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Mouse study, 2023</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The trial most often cited gave 40 healthy Asian men and women aged 35 to 64 either 120mg of
        hyaluronic acid a day or placebo for 12 weeks. The hyaluronic acid group improved on wrinkle
        assessment, water content of the outer skin layer, water loss through the skin and elasticity.
        That is a small trial, and most of the others in this field are similar in size.
      </p>

      <h2 id="absorb">Does oral hyaluronic acid get absorbed?</h2>

      <p>
        Only a little, and not in the way you might expect. Hyaluronic acid is a large molecule, and it is
        not simply absorbed and delivered to the skin intact.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2023 study in mice found that gut bacteria, particularly <em>Bacteroides</em> species, were
        essential for uptake. They break hyaluronic acid into small fragments, some of which pass through
        the gut wall. The researchers estimated bioavailability at about 0.2%, and suggested that any
        benefit is more likely to come from those fragments acting as signals in the body than from
        hyaluronic acid physically reaching the skin. An older 2014 rat study using labelled hyaluronic
        acid did find the label concentrating in skin, and reported much higher absorption, so the animal
        evidence disagrees. Either way, it is animal evidence, and human absorption data are limited.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is also why oral hyaluronic acid, like oral collagen, works slowly if it works at all. You are
        sending a signal over weeks, not topping up a reservoir.
      </p>

      <h2 id="dose">How much oral hyaluronic acid should you take?</h2>

      <p>
        The dose with the clearest trial support is 120mg a day for at least 12 weeks. There is no
        established benefit from taking more.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Product</th><th scope="col">Hyaluronic acid per daily serving</th><th scope="col">Against the 120mg trial dose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Collabloom</th><td>100mg</td><td>Slightly below</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Skelecoll</th><td>120mg</td><td>Matches (formulated for joints)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        We should be plain about our own label. Collabloom contains 100mg of hyaluronic acid per shot,
        about 17% below the 120mg used in the best-known skin trial. It sits within the range that has
        been studied, but it is not the trial dose, and we would rather you knew that than assumed it.
        Hyaluronic acid is one of several ingredients in Collabloom, alongside 8,000mg of hydrolysed marine
        collagen; whether the combination does more than the parts has not been tested. Skelecoll's 120mg
        matches the trial figure, though Skelecoll is formulated for joints, not skin.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most people tolerate oral hyaluronic acid well in trials. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding, or
        managing a medical condition, check with your doctor first, as you would with any supplement. For
        a wider view of safety, see <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side effects</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="serum">Oral vs topical hyaluronic acid</h2>

      <p>
        They do different jobs. A hyaluronic acid serum is a humectant: it draws water into the outer
        layer of skin and makes it look plumper while you are wearing it, and the effect fades when you
        stop. It works within minutes and needs no absorption into the body.
      </p>

      <p>
        Oral hyaluronic acid aims at skin from the inside, over weeks, through the signalling route
        described above. The evidence for it is thinner than for a well-formulated serum, and no trial we
        found compared the two head to head. If dry or dehydrated skin is your main problem, a serum under
        a moisturiser, plus daily sunscreen, is the place to start. A supplement is an optional addition,
        not a replacement. Our guide to <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a> covers
        how the oral options compare.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ceramides">What about oral ceramides?</h2>

      <p>
        Interesting, but even earlier in the evidence than hyaluronic acid. Ceramides are fats that hold
        the skin barrier together, and some small trials have tested them taken by mouth.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A 6-week trial in 51 adults found 100mg a day of a konjac extract, containing 5mg of glycosylceramides, reduced skin dryness, redness and itching against placebo. One author was affiliated with the extract's maker.</li>
        <li>A wheat ceramide study reported a large rise in measured skin hydration on the arms, but no difference from placebo on the dermatologists' own assessment.</li>
        <li>Researchers in the field describe oral ceramide studies in people as scarce.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Collabloom contains 30mg of oral ceramides. Because the trials used different sources and
        reported different fractions, such as 5mg of glycosylceramides within 100mg of extract, the 30mg
        figure cannot be compared directly with a trial dose, and we will not pretend it can.
      </p>

