      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does oral hyaluronic acid work for skin?</summary>
          <p>Probably a little. A 2025 meta-analysis of 7 randomised trials found significant improvements in skin hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth, but not in firmness or wrinkle volume. The trials were small and varied.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much oral hyaluronic acid should I take?</summary>
          <p>The best-known trial used 120mg a day for 12 weeks. There is no evidence that higher doses work better. Collabloom contains 100mg, slightly below that trial dose.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is oral hyaluronic acid absorbed?</summary>
          <p>Only partly. In a 2023 mouse study, gut bacteria broke it into small fragments and bioavailability was estimated at about 0.2%. Any benefit is thought to come from those fragments acting as signals, not from intact hyaluronic acid reaching the skin.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is oral or topical hyaluronic acid better?</summary>
          <p>They do different jobs. A serum hydrates the skin surface within minutes while you wear it. A supplement aims to work from within over weeks and has thinner evidence. A serum plus sunscreen is the better starting point for dry skin.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does oral hyaluronic acid take to work?</summary>
          <p>Trials measured results after 6 to 12 weeks. Expect to take it daily for at least 12 weeks before judging whether it has helped.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take hyaluronic acid with collagen?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Many supplements combine them, including Collabloom. Each has its own small trial evidence, but whether the combination works better than either alone has not been tested.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do oral ceramides work?</summary>
          <p>The evidence is early. Small trials report less dryness and better hydration, but human studies are scarce and doses are reported differently, so they are hard to compare.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

